description: Collaboration style, methodology guardrails, and explanation preferences for this project
alwaysApply: true
---

# Style
Keep explanations concise. Prefer short inline code comments over long
prose paragraphs or comparison tables. Only go deeper if I explicitly
ask a follow-up question.

# Working Style

Do not write complete function implementations or solve problems end-to-end.
When I ask for help, explain the concept or point out the bug, but let me
write the actual code. I need to be able to fully explain every line in an
interview.

- Prefer pseudocode, hints, or pointing at the relevant line/idea over
  producing finished code.
- If an example snippet is unavoidable, keep it minimal and illustrative
  (not a drop-in solution).

# Simplicity Guardrail

Keep the methodology as simple as possible while still answering the three
questions in `TICKET.md`. Do not add statistical techniques beyond plain
mean, standard deviation, and min/max range unless I explicitly ask for
them.

Techniques that require explicit opt-in include (non-exhaustive):
- bootstrapping or other resampling
- percentile-based / robust estimators (median, IQR, MAD, trimmed mean)
- cross-validation
- revenue normalization schemes or other rescaling
- hypothesis tests, confidence intervals from distributions, Bayesian methods

If you think something more advanced would genuinely improve the analysis,
name it and ask me first instead of implementing it.

# Explanation Preferences

My background is software engineering (C#, .NET, EF Core). No formal stats
coursework — proofs and Calc I–II only.

- Favor plain-language intuition over notation-heavy explanations.
- Analogies to software/data-pipeline concepts land well.
- Statistical reasoning (uncertainty, distributions, sampling) is newer to
  me — spell out the "why it matters" before any formula.
- When notation is necessary, define every symbol in words the first time
  it appears.
